WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.380
How do we choose a

2
00:00:01.380 --> 00:00:03.660
correct state and algorithmic strategy?

3
00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:06.080
We will compare divide and

4
00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:08.740
conquer, greedy and dynamic programming,

5
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:10.620
then use a path example

6
00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:12.280
to test whether a proposed

7
00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:14.340
state remembers enough information.

8
00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:17.340
Divide and conquer solves smaller

9
00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:19.300
versions and combines their answers,

10
00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:21.040
as merge sort solves two

11
00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:23.280
halves and merges them. Greedy

12
00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:25.200
instead commits to one locally

13
00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:27.220
best feasible choice, and needs

14
00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:28.480
a proof that this choice

15
00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:29.920
can safely agree with an

16
00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:30.360
optimum.

17
00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:34.240
Dynamic programming compares relevant alternatives

18
00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:36.140
while reusing stored answers for

19
00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:38.180
overlapping states. It may be

20
00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:40.480
recursive or iterative, and it

21
00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:41.560
does not need to try

22
00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:43.700
every numerical split unless that

23
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:45.740
problem's recurrence actually demands it.

24
00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:47.740
Start by defining a state

25
00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:48.900
in a sentence rather than

26
00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:50.760
writing a table shape. The

27
00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:52.620
state must contain everything needed

28
00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:54.000
to determine the best future

29
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.380
answer independently of the particular

30
00:00:56.380 --> 00:00:57.600
history that reached it.

31
00:00:58.100 --> 00:00:59.940
For minimum coin change with

32
00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:02.080
unlimited coins, the remaining amount

33
00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:04.800
is enough. For zero-one knapsack

34
00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:05.940
it is not enough, because

35
00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:07.620
the available item set changes;

36
00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:10.020
the standard prefix formulation therefore

37
00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:11.920
also records how many initial

38
00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:13.100
items remain eligible.

39
00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:16.500
Optimal substructure can be justified

40
00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:18.920
by replacement: if an optimum

41
00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:21.620
used a nonoptimal subsolution, a

42
00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:23.480
better legal replacement would improve

43
00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:25.360
the whole answer. The word

44
00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:27.580
legal matters, because a replacement

45
00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:29.580
that violates the original constraints

46
00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:30.540
proves nothing.

47
00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:33.220
Consider a shortest path split

48
00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:34.900
into a prefix and suffix

49
00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:36.660
under conditions where the shortest

50
00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:39.280
route is well-defined. Replacing a

51
00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:40.880
subpath by a cheaper route

52
00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:42.540
cannot improve on a true

53
00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:44.940
optimum, which motivates the familiar

54
00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:46.320
shortest-path property.

55
00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:49.320
Now consider longest simple paths,

56
00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:51.400
where no vertex may repeat.

57
00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:53.360
A state naming only the

58
00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:55.460
current endpoint forgets which vertices

59
00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:57.440
have already been used, even

60
00:01:57.440 --> 00:01:59.260
though those choices decide which

61
00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:00.720
continuations are legal.

62
00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:03.580
Suppose two partial paths reach

63
00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:05.340
the same vertex, but one

64
00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:06.700
has already used a vertex

65
00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:08.260
required by a later extension.

66
00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:10.400
Merging those histories into one

67
00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:12.440
endpoint-only answer can keep a

68
00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:14.760
numerically attractive path whose future

69
00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:15.900
extension is forbidden.

70
00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:18.100
Adding a visited set can

71
00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:19.900
restore the missing information in

72
00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:23.500
a longest-simple-path formulation. However, n

73
00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:25.000
vertices have two to the

74
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:27.160
n possible subsets, so a

75
00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:28.960
sufficient state does not automatically

76
00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:31.580
produce a polynomial-size dynamic program.

77
00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:34.100
The same discipline explains our

78
00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:36.880
greedy examples. Fractional density ordering

79
00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:38.740
has a legal equal-weight exchange,

80
00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:41.140
while indivisible knapsack and arbitrary

81
00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:43.240
coin denominations can defeat a

82
00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:45.440
local choice, so each requires

83
00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:46.880
its own proof or counterexample.

84
00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:49.620
After deriving the state and

85
00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:51.940
recurrence, give an evaluation order

86
00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:54.720
that respects dependencies. Then multiply

87
00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:56.120
the number of distinct states

88
00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:58.040
by transition work, and add

89
00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:01.340
initialisation, reconstruction and stack costs

90
00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:02.140
where they matter.

91
00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:04.200
Things to take note of:

92
00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:06.180
an algorithmic label is not

93
00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:08.260
a correctness proof, and optimal

94
00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:10.420
substructure alone does not guarantee

95
00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:12.600
a small table. Keep every

96
00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:14.140
relevant constraint in the state,

97
00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:16.420
distinguish reachable states from repeated

98
00:03:16.420 --> 00:03:18.100
calls, and let the actual

99
00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:21.320
dependencies choose your implementation, obviously.
